From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:50:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: venus: msm8998: power the vcodec subcores before
 touching VBIF

The msm8998 wedge is one write -- 0x00000003 to base+0x80124, the first VBIF
register, from venus_set_registers(). Reading the power state at exactly that
point, with venus stopped just after core_power(POWER_ON):

	video_subcore1   off-0
	video_subcore0   off-0
	video_top        on
	  cc00000.video-codec   active

Both subcores are unpowered and the driver writes into VBIF regardless. That is
a NoC timeout by construction, and it explains why no reordering helped: with
presets_early=1, writing them before venus_boot() wedges just the same.

mainline attaches the subcore GDSCs to the video-decoder/video-encoder child
nodes, which nothing powers at probe -- venus_dec and venus_enc are not even
loaded. The vendor does not do this. Its pil-tz venus node carries three
regulators, gdsc_venus, gdsc_venus_core0 and gdsc_venus_core1, and
__enable_regulators() brings all three up inside __venus_power_on(), before
__set_registers() touches anything.

Put the subcore domains and their clocks on the parent, as sdm845 and sc7180
already do, so core_power_v1() calls vcodec_domains_enable() before any
register access. The children keep their own references; a genpd may have more
than one consumer.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi    | 14 ++++++++++----
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 34 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi
@@ -3175,7 +3175,10 @@
 			compatible = "qcom,msm8998-venus";
 			reg = <0x0cc00000 0xff000>;
 			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 287 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
-			power-domains = <&mmcc VIDEO_TOP_GDSC>;
+			power-domains = <&mmcc VIDEO_TOP_GDSC>,
+					<&mmcc VIDEO_SUBCORE0_GDSC>,
+					<&mmcc VIDEO_SUBCORE1_GDSC>;
+			power-domain-names = "venus", "vcodec0", "vcodec1";
 			clocks = <&mmcc VIDEO_CORE_CLK>,
 				 <&mmcc VIDEO_AHB_CLK>,
 				 <&mmcc VIDEO_AXI_CLK>,
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/core.c
@@ -881,7 +881,30 @@
 	.clks_num = 6,
 	.vcodec0_clks = { "core" },
 	.vcodec1_clks = { "core" },
-	.vcodec_clks_num = 1,
+	/*
+	 * Measured at the point of the wedge, with venus stopped just after
+	 * core_power(POWER_ON):
+	 *
+	 *   video_subcore1   off-0
+	 *   video_subcore0   off-0
+	 *   video_top        on
+	 *
+	 * Both subcores are unpowered, and venus_set_registers() writes into
+	 * VBIF anyway. Declaring the vcodec pmdomains is what makes
+	 * core_power_v1() call vcodec_domains_enable() first -- which is what
+	 * the vendor does: its pil-tz venus node carries gdsc_venus,
+	 * gdsc_venus_core0 and gdsc_venus_core1, and __enable_regulators()
+	 * brings all three up before __set_registers().
+	 *
+	 * Only the DOMAINS are claimed here, not their clocks. The subcore
+	 * GDSCs are HW_CTRL, so software cannot force video_subcore0_clk on --
+	 * it reports "status stuck at 'off'" and core_power_v1() fails -EBUSY.
+	 * Leaving vcodec_clks unset makes vcodec_clks_enable() walk NULLs and
+	 * no-op, while vcodec_domains_enable() still powers the subcores. The
+	 * per-session clocks stay where they belong, on the child nodes.
+	 */
+	.vcodec_pmdomains = (const char *[]) { "venus", "vcodec0", "vcodec1" },
+	.vcodec_pmdomains_num = 3,
 	.max_load = 2563200,
 	.hfi_version = HFI_VERSION_3XX,
 	.vmem_id = VIDC_RESOURCE_NONE,
